             Documentation for Various Tektronix DemosThe programs contained here are some random demos that will run if you have aTektronix 4010/4014 compatible graphics terminal. If you do not, you may beable to port them to your graphics environment, as the line-drawing routineswere abstracted away into a separate program, and you shoulkd be able toreplace the functions that are called with your own.Cam:  This program is a part of a program I am (was) working on. This programallows you to enter in an engine camshaft lift profile numerically, and itwill plot a lift curve and show a profile of the lobe. Eventually, it willshow the entire camshaft in 3-d, and will compute lift and duration data. Thefirst prompt asks for the ratio at which you are entering the lift data. Ifyou are entering the lift at the cam, enter 1.00.If you are entering the netlift at the rocker, enter the ratio of the rocker. Then, enter the lift valuesin inches. Finally, enter the base circle diameter of the cam. For aChevrolet, this is usually 1.25 inches. For an interesting example, enter thefollowing data into the program:Rocker arm ratio: 1.5lift @ -180 degrees: 0lift @ -160 degrees: 0lift @ -140 degrees: 0lift @ -120 degrees: 0.02lift @ -100 degrees: 0.06lift @ -80 degrees: 0.16lift @ -60 degrees: 0.25lift @ -40 degrees: 0.33lift @ -20 degrees: 0.375lift @ 0 degrees: 0.390lift @ 20 degrees: 0.375lift @ 40 degrees: 0.33lift @ 60 degrees: 0.25lift @ 80 degrees: 0.16lift @ 100 degrees: 0.06lift @ 120 degrees: 0.02lift @ 140 degrees: 0lift @ 160 degrees: 0lift @ 180 degrees: 0Base Circle: 1.25Then, after viewing the lift curve and the lobe profile, enter 114 as the lobecenterline angle.Lissaj:This program displays Lissajous figures. If you are familiar with these, thisprogram is relatively straightforward. However, instead of entering x and yfrequencies, you enter a step value. A good start value set is:X-step: .1Y-step: .2Duration: 500phi: 0This will produce a figure-8 shaped curve, as most electronic techniciansshould be familiar with. entering different x and y step values will producedifferent patterns.Lisrot:Same as lissaj, but entering a phi increment and a # repetitions allows"animation". The duration value specified tells how many segments aredisplayed before the screen is cleared and phi is incremented. # repetitionsindicates how long you want it to run.A good set of starting values is:x-incr: .1y-incr: .1Duration: 64#repetitions:20phi increment: .125Lorenz3d:This is an implementation of the Lorenz attractor, a model of a chaotic systemin nature. Good starting values are:Z-mag: 15Z-offset: 16dt=.2a=5b=15c=1playing around with the a,b,and c values changes the pattern produced. Dtspecifies the time step between each point. The z mag and z offset positionthe image on the screen and affect its size.Wgen:Wgen is a simplistic waveform synthesizer. By entering a time step >=1 andamplitudes of various harmonics of a sine wave, it displays the correspondingwaveform through one cycle. A good starting set of values is:Step=3Fund.=30second=50third=0fourth=0fifth=60sixth=30seventh=40eighth=0sixteenth=20This is also a braindead program, but its a neat demo.In the future, I hope to add frequency modulation as well as phase-shifting soi can use the numeric output to create a wavetable to be used on a D-to-Aconvertor to do music.Tekfuncs.c, tekfuncs.h:These are the line-drawing functions called by all theTektronix demos. They basically take line endpoints and convert to theappropriate control sequences for a 4010/4014 terminal and output them tostdout.Any questions/comments/ideas to:Russell Hoffmanrh2y+@andrew.cmu.eduCarnegie Mellon UniversityAny flames, gripes, etc to: /nil